Every function starts with \code{kits_} (kit scale), with the same names, main arguments, columns and printout as kits,
so the two packages can be read side by side. A few arguments differ (for example the defaults of
\code{\link[=kits_linking]{kits_linking()}}, and \verb{engine =} of \code{\link[=kits_item]{kits_item()}}); the README lists them.
The data are a response matrix, as for \code{dexter::fit_enorm()}
(one row per person-wave, one column per item, NA means not administered),
or a long data.frame (\code{visit_id}, \code{item_id}, \code{item_score}).
}
\section{Names}{

kitsPreview uses the names of kits 0.23.0:
\itemize{
\item \code{visit_id}: one child at one visit (a person-wave), the row names of the response matrix;
\code{subj_id}: the child (the same in every wave).
\item item pattern (\code{pattern_id}): which items a person-wave answered; the standard form of a wave
is the item pattern of most of its children. The raw score is \code{raw}, its maximum \code{raw_max}.
\item A link: new value = \code{intercept} + \code{slope} x old value (Kolen and Brennan, 2014, call the slope
A and the intercept B).
